{
  "dom_id": "step-q334160",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =5$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $Δx=0.25$\n\nIII. $Δx=0.3$\n\nIV. $Δx=1.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$",
  "worked_solution": "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =5,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.1$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=1.5$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.1,$ $Δx=0.25,$ and $Δx=0.3.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
